[{:db/id "q-337547/selection/9eb40e5df9dcefe02640238eacc7bf86da27a7c45ae3ac2124903e2e62c6f926" :answer/id #uuid "59673c0f-1dc1-5444-bb62-100eaac73c02" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-4{e}^{2x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-337547/selection/d60064e90ead9557d9bbe40fb679fb28d43f026cf62b6bd96a515014c1d4cbdc" :answer/id #uuid "b6fd716d-4be4-57e3-ba16-9a2b4f190b58" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-2x{e}^{2x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-337547/selection/c34a0d98c7ea154bfb56363989018d9d21f945a61affcb84d6d0176cdcfcdddc" :answer/id #uuid "08d29d06-7b60-5b53-8826-b51924725406" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-x{e}^{2x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-337547/selection/a78251ad0164495d514cbd768a333b6b8b83514ed0123c20cd30ec62a1084a09" :answer/id #uuid "57528d90-9d29-59c4-bd1a-011b766fa35b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-{e}^{2x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-337547/selection/98e4076f46747fd598acb21e5bf099a12227f9bb2b150365e2ee1ca64b9bf377" :answer/id #uuid "dc68a939-1bc8-5d5a-b2af-5ddf895e38d4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-2{e}^{2x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-337547/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "18ed1ff4-eccd-5722-b753-253b967edebb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-337547/selection/a78251ad0164495d514cbd768a333b6b8b83514ed0123c20cd30ec62a1084a09" :answer-field/choices ["q-337547/selection/9eb40e5df9dcefe02640238eacc7bf86da27a7c45ae3ac2124903e2e62c6f926" "q-337547/selection/d60064e90ead9557d9bbe40fb679fb28d43f026cf62b6bd96a515014c1d4cbdc" "q-337547/selection/c34a0d98c7ea154bfb56363989018d9d21f945a61affcb84d6d0176cdcfcdddc" "q-337547/selection/a78251ad0164495d514cbd768a333b6b8b83514ed0123c20cd30ec62a1084a09" "q-337547/selection/98e4076f46747fd598acb21e5bf099a12227f9bb2b150365e2ee1ca64b9bf377"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-337547" :question/id #uuid "06701a13-3cbb-58f0-8fe6-88db9f0d26c9" :question/math-academy-id "q-337547" :question/problem "Consider the following differential equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}-4{y}^{′}+4y=\\frac{{e}^{2x}}{{x}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nThe general solution to this equation can be written as\n\n$$\ny(x)=A{e}^{2x}+Bx{e}^{2x}+g(x),\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ and $B$ are constants. Which of the following could be the function $g(x)?$\n\n*Hint: Use the variation of parameters method.*" :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have a second-order linear differential equation with linearly independent fundamental solutions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x).$ Then, according to the method of variation of parameters, we can seek a particular solution ${y}_{p}$ using the ansatz\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}(x) & ={u}_{1}(x){y}_{1}(x)+{u}_{2}(x){y}_{2}(x),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${u}_{1}$ and ${u}_{2}$ satisfy the following system:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}^{′}{y}_{1}^{′}+{u}_{2}^{′}{y}_{2}^{′}=f(x) \\\\ {u}_{1}^{′}{y}_{1}+{u}_{2}^{′}{y}_{2}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solution to this system is\n\n$$\n{u}_{1}(x)=-\\int \\frac{f(x){y}_{2}(x)}{W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})}\\,\\text{d}x,\\,{u}_{2}(x)=\\int \\frac{f(x){y}_{1}(x)}{W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})}\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nwhere $W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})$ is the Wronskian.\n\nFrom the given information, we see that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{2x}+Bx{e}^{2x}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the fundamental solutions to the corresponding homogeneous equation are\n\n$$\n{y}_{1}(x)={e}^{2x},\\,{y}_{2}(x)=x{e}^{2x}.\n$$\n\nWe can use the method of variation of parameters if these solutions are independent. So, let's first compute the Wronskian:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({y}_{1},{y}_{2}) & =|\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{2}^{′}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & ={y}_{1}{y}_{2}^{′}-{y}_{1}^{′}{y}_{2} \\\\  & =({e}^{2x})(x{e}^{2x})^{′}-({e}^{2x})^{′}(x{e}^{2x}) \\\\  & =({e}^{2x})({e}^{2x}+2x{e}^{2x})-(2{e}^{2x})(x{e}^{2x}) \\\\  & ={e}^{4x}+2x{e}^{4x}-2x{e}^{4x} \\\\  & ={e}^{4x}\\ne 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the Wronskian is not equal to zero, this confirms that the functions  ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ are independent. \n\nLet's compute ${u}_{1}(x)$ and ${u}_{2}(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}(x) & =-\\int \\frac{{e}^{2x}\\cdot x{e}^{2x}}{{x}^{2}{e}^{4x}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\int \\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x| \\\\ {u}_{2}(x) & =\\int \\frac{{e}^{2x}\\cdot {e}^{2x}}{{x}^{2}{e}^{4x}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}(x) & ={u}_{1}{y}_{1}+{u}_{2}{y}_{2} \\\\  & =(-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|)({e}^{2x})+(-\\frac{1}{x})(x{e}^{2x}) \\\\  & =-{e}^{2x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|+1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe general solution is the sum of the complementary and particular solutions, and can be written as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(x) & ={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x) \\\\  & =A{e}^{2x}+Bx{e}^{2x}-{e}^{2x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|+1) \\\\  & =(A-1){e}^{2x}+Bx{e}^{2x}-{e}^{2x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we redefine the arbitrary constant $A$ as\n\n$$\nA:=(A-1)\n$$\n\nour general solution becomes\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =A{e}^{2x}+Bx{e}^{2x}{{\\,-\\,{e}^{2x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|}_{⏟}}_{g(x)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\ng(x)=-{e}^{2x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-337547/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b165305b-42f4-584a-9f8f-9455e5a05a9e"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-337547"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-337541/selection/3c535691947d063096b8b36c1dcce655c5590f07a0e505a188418b30e470cc8d" :answer/id #uuid "7474064a-cb39-5f0a-9caf-75adbcdb96bb" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-({x}^{2}+\\frac{2x}{7}){e}^{-4x}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-337541/selection/574a62f01031032fcd79b9fd05cb787635c190cce4a683d611b76ef2545cf664" :answer/id #uuid "69c42a36-92cb-5c22-a1cc-c59b419b1499" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-({x}^{2}+\\frac{x}{3}){e}^{-4x}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-337541/selection/b2c6c375df29c5ee91497b53e28a82825c40acaa96ef7c73003f3cf3bfa24134" :answer/id #uuid "520cd96c-5614-53aa-94c8-f37070a8c7d5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-({x}^{2}+\\frac{3x}{7}){e}^{-4x}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-337541/selection/0638f9d1fcc7e1d035a5fcc6714590bf704c5a0c7bb0d3c11f32a15d52325f12" :answer/id #uuid "8774dfe6-cbe4-57fb-86ab-ce8b60c2e599" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-({x}^{2}+\\frac{2x}{5}){e}^{-4x}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-337541/selection/fc20984699eb015bd205a65bbf6205ea9854a1a948a74c534079902735f91c7e" :answer/id #uuid "8edcf2f4-928e-56a3-9b02-60b50bcc082b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-({x}^{2}+\\frac{x}{7}){e}^{-4x}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-337541/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "d4a84989-00c3-5ac9-9165-ba409c0d8565" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-337541/selection/3c535691947d063096b8b36c1dcce655c5590f07a0e505a188418b30e470cc8d" :answer-field/choices ["q-337541/selection/3c535691947d063096b8b36c1dcce655c5590f07a0e505a188418b30e470cc8d" "q-337541/selection/574a62f01031032fcd79b9fd05cb787635c190cce4a683d611b76ef2545cf664" "q-337541/selection/b2c6c375df29c5ee91497b53e28a82825c40acaa96ef7c73003f3cf3bfa24134" "q-337541/selection/0638f9d1fcc7e1d035a5fcc6714590bf704c5a0c7bb0d3c11f32a15d52325f12" "q-337541/selection/fc20984699eb015bd205a65bbf6205ea9854a1a948a74c534079902735f91c7e"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-337541" :question/id #uuid "f6c167fc-51f7-5156-879d-32c227a0d2b8" :question/math-academy-id "q-337541" :question/problem "Consider the following differential equation.\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+{y}^{′}-12y=14x{e}^{-4x}\n$$\n\nThe general solution to this equation can be written as\n\n$$\ny(x)=A{e}^{-4x}+B{e}^{3x}+g(x),\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ and $B$ are constants. Which of the following could be the function $g(x)?$\n\n*Hint: Use the variation of parameters method. You may also wish to make use of the following result (integration constants omitted):*\n\n$$\n\\int x{e}^{-7x}\\,\\text{d}x=-\\frac{1}{49}(7x+1){e}^{-7x}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have a second-order linear differential equation with linearly independent fundamental solutions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x).$ Then, according to the method of variation of parameters, we can seek a particular solution ${y}_{p}$ using the ansatz\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}(x) & ={u}_{1}(x){y}_{1}(x)+{u}_{2}(x){y}_{2}(x),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${u}_{1}$ and ${u}_{2}$ satisfy the following system:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}^{′}{y}_{1}^{′}+{u}_{2}^{′}{y}_{2}^{′}=f(x) \\\\ {u}_{1}^{′}{y}_{1}+{u}_{2}^{′}{y}_{2}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solution to this system is\n\n$$\n{u}_{1}(x)=-\\int \\frac{f(x){y}_{2}(x)}{W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})}\\,\\text{d}x,\\,{u}_{2}(x)=\\int \\frac{f(x){y}_{1}(x)}{W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})}\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nwhere $W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})$ is the Wronskian.\n\nFirst, we note that the forcing term is $f(x)=14x{e}^{-4x}.$\n\nFrom the given information, we see that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{-4x}+B{e}^{3x}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the fundamental solutions to the corresponding homogeneous equation are\n\n$$\n{y}_{1}(x)={e}^{-4x},\\,{y}_{2}(x)={e}^{3x}.\n$$\n\nWe can use the method of variation of parameters if these solutions are independent. So, let's first compute the Wronskian:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({y}_{1},{y}_{2}) & =|\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{2}^{′}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & ={y}_{1}{y}_{2}^{′}-{y}_{1}^{′}{y}_{2} \\\\  & =({e}^{-4x})({e}^{3x})^{′}-({e}^{-4x})^{′}({e}^{3x}) \\\\  & =({e}^{-4x})(3{e}^{3x})-(-4{e}^{-4x})({e}^{3x}) \\\\  & =3{e}^{-x}+4{e}^{-x} \\\\  & =7{e}^{-x}\\ne 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the Wronskian is not equal to zero, this confirms that the functions  ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ are independent. \n\nLet's compute ${u}_{1}(x)$ and ${u}_{2}(x)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}(x) & =-\\int \\frac{14x{e}^{-4x}\\cdot {e}^{3x}}{7{e}^{-x}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-2\\int x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-{x}^{2} \\\\ {u}_{2}(x) & =\\int \\frac{14x{e}^{-4x}\\cdot {e}^{-4x}}{7{e}^{-x}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\int x{e}^{-7x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{49}(7x+1){e}^{-7x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}(x) & ={u}_{1}{y}_{1}+{u}_{2}{y}_{2} \\\\  & =(-{x}^{2})({e}^{-4x})+[-\\frac{2}{49}(7x+1){e}^{-7x}]({e}^{3x}) \\\\  & =-{x}^{2}{e}^{-4x}-\\frac{2}{49}(7x+1){e}^{-4x} \\\\  & =(-{x}^{2}-\\frac{2x}{7}-\\frac{2}{49}){e}^{-4x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe general solution is the sum of the complementary and particular solutions, and can be written as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(x) & ={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x) \\\\  & =A{e}^{-4x}+B{e}^{3x}+(-{x}^{2}-\\frac{2x}{7}-\\frac{2}{49}){e}^{-4x} \\\\  & =(A-\\frac{2}{49}){e}^{-4x}+B{e}^{3x}-({x}^{2}+\\frac{2x}{7}){e}^{-4x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we redefine the arbitrary constant $A$ as\n\n$$\nA:=(A-\\frac{2}{49})\n$$\n\nour general solution becomes\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =A{e}^{-4x}+B{e}^{3x}{{-({x}^{2}+\\frac{2x}{7}){e}^{-4x}}_{⏟}}_{g(x)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\ng(x)=-({x}^{2}+\\frac{2x}{7}){e}^{-4x}.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-337541/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b165305b-42f4-584a-9f8f-9455e5a05a9e"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-337541"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-337548/selection/b3cc144ad80152f89550765381109d5597b222a7377df6f9d2289461bf5abd7a" :answer/id #uuid "18848514-50d8-5bc9-8226-5c35e03f3e86" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{-x}}{2x}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-337548/selection/e2d507271041a542c9725a1bb6126ccf5927f960c90c38c3b3db0b81b1993912" :answer/id #uuid "64f1e5c2-cf7e-54a2-a869-6d7a8e4ca2e1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{-x}}{x}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-337548/selection/0bea6ff8c28be69a61172e2a61b08e1cce631aa62531012a9cc270e9cb46638d" :answer/id #uuid "2589c8fe-af19-59fd-a747-36c7a120e107" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{-x}}{4{x}^{2}}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-337548/selection/6f4421369266b1ba6691fb6e16e48c27f3964e8da209e59d25a9e6ca6e1ccb97" :answer/id #uuid "f7580637-c1de-5426-8cb5-844da030df7e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{-x}}{4x}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-337548/selection/0b9316dd4795876be8f8c7d51063ddc1d92edfc125e66d58114dbcd39eb2fedd" :answer/id #uuid "128a80d7-7962-53e6-a915-c0efb833a234" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{-x}}{{x}^{2}}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-337548/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "85982ca7-fc0e-5099-b92e-f7ca3c9ae60e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-337548/selection/b3cc144ad80152f89550765381109d5597b222a7377df6f9d2289461bf5abd7a" :answer-field/choices ["q-337548/selection/b3cc144ad80152f89550765381109d5597b222a7377df6f9d2289461bf5abd7a" "q-337548/selection/e2d507271041a542c9725a1bb6126ccf5927f960c90c38c3b3db0b81b1993912" "q-337548/selection/0bea6ff8c28be69a61172e2a61b08e1cce631aa62531012a9cc270e9cb46638d" "q-337548/selection/6f4421369266b1ba6691fb6e16e48c27f3964e8da209e59d25a9e6ca6e1ccb97" "q-337548/selection/0b9316dd4795876be8f8c7d51063ddc1d92edfc125e66d58114dbcd39eb2fedd"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-337548" :question/id #uuid "000d5951-3272-57cb-a70c-1551b6af9160" :question/math-academy-id "q-337548" :question/problem "Consider the following differential equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+2{y}^{′}+y=\\frac{{e}^{-x}}{{x}^{3}}.\n$$\n\nThe general solution to this equation can be written as\n\n$$\ny(x)=A{e}^{-x}+Bx{e}^{-x}+g(x),\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ and $B$ are constants. Which of the following could be the function $g(x)?$\n\n*Hint: Use the variation of parameters method.*" :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have a second-order linear differential equation with linearly independent fundamental solutions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x).$ Then, according to the method of variation of parameters, we can seek a particular solution ${y}_{p}$ using the ansatz\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}(x) & ={u}_{1}(x){y}_{1}(x)+{u}_{2}(x){y}_{2}(x),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${u}_{1}$ and ${u}_{2}$ satisfy the following system:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}^{′}{y}_{1}^{′}+{u}_{2}^{′}{y}_{2}^{′}=f(x) \\\\ {u}_{1}^{′}{y}_{1}+{u}_{2}^{′}{y}_{2}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solution to this system is\n\n$$\n{u}_{1}(x)=-\\int \\frac{f(x){y}_{2}(x)}{W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})}\\,\\text{d}x,\\,{u}_{2}(x)=\\int \\frac{f(x){y}_{1}(x)}{W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})}\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nwhere $W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})$ is the Wronskian.\n\nFrom the given information, we see that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{-x}+Bx{e}^{-x}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the fundamental solutions to the corresponding homogeneous equation are\n\n$$\n{y}_{1}(x)={e}^{-x},\\,{y}_{2}(x)=x{e}^{-x}.\n$$\n\nWe can use the method of variation of parameters if these solutions are independent. So, let's first compute the Wronskian:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({y}_{1},{y}_{2}) & =|\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{2}^{′}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & ={y}_{1}{y}_{2}^{′}-{y}_{1}^{′}{y}_{2} \\\\  & =({e}^{-x})(x{e}^{-x})^{′}-({e}^{-x})^{′}(x{e}^{-x}) \\\\  & =({e}^{-x})({e}^{-x}-x{e}^{-x})-(-{e}^{-x})(x{e}^{-x}) \\\\  & ={e}^{-2x}-x{e}^{-2x}+x{e}^{-2x} \\\\  & ={e}^{-2x}\\ne 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the Wronskian is not equal to zero, this confirms that the functions  ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ are independent.\n\nLet's compute ${u}_{1}(x)$ and ${u}_{2}(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}(x) & =-\\int \\frac{{e}^{-x}\\cdot x{e}^{-x}}{{x}^{3}{e}^{-2x}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{x} \\\\ {u}_{2}(x) & =\\int \\frac{{e}^{-x}\\cdot {e}^{-x}}{{x}^{3}{e}^{-2x}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2{x}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}(x) & ={u}_{1}{y}_{1}+{u}_{2}{y}_{2} \\\\  & =(\\frac{1}{x}){e}^{-x}+(-\\frac{1}{2{x}^{2}})x{e}^{-x} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{-x}}{x}-\\frac{{e}^{-x}}{2x} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{-x}}{2x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe general solution is the sum of the complementary and particular solutions, and can be written as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(x) & ={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x) \\\\  & =A{e}^{-x}+Bx{e}^{-x}+{{\\frac{{e}^{-x}}{2x}}_{⏟}}_{g(x)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\ng(x)=\\frac{{e}^{-x}}{2x}.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-337548/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b165305b-42f4-584a-9f8f-9455e5a05a9e"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-337548"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-337542/selection/7bf9010b4f86f72788c506094c67e6965cd5574c567291a081c5fd623817244a" :answer/id #uuid "66298ada-d1cf-5925-8d6c-443d5d2e8f5e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-({x}^{2}+2x){e}^{2x}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-337542/selection/c3bcff4a74b143b41815753e04486f488c37facdb0487f060c089dfafb91385f" :answer/id #uuid "3f970cb6-ba77-55eb-a1c1-a7fa84216e25" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-({x}^{2}+4x){e}^{2x}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-337542/selection/3d7a391dce2222c09529616d5b0fd79ae6729613ce03438e6b7e9ab786d64e1b" :answer/id #uuid "ede90880-a39e-51e4-afb6-27741eaf96b8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-(2{x}^{2}+4x){e}^{2x}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-337542/selection/1cf7ad2c8ba9afef7858414203f4593b25338cbc444eff9f31531226939ba2f2" :answer/id #uuid "803505c1-22fa-5e6a-ad9c-b4a70555a4cf" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-({x}^{3}+2x){e}^{2x}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-337542/selection/2929e3aa7077b26de26ed6f12808d2703d5dc866106455fbc861551c29f67c16" :answer/id #uuid "70b48aab-4042-5c20-ade9-5665d9c9c5c8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-(2{x}^{2}+2x){e}^{2x}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-337542/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "74379a5f-fc24-5ff9-b492-4cab6807d2e8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-337542/selection/7bf9010b4f86f72788c506094c67e6965cd5574c567291a081c5fd623817244a" :answer-field/choices ["q-337542/selection/7bf9010b4f86f72788c506094c67e6965cd5574c567291a081c5fd623817244a" "q-337542/selection/c3bcff4a74b143b41815753e04486f488c37facdb0487f060c089dfafb91385f" "q-337542/selection/3d7a391dce2222c09529616d5b0fd79ae6729613ce03438e6b7e9ab786d64e1b" "q-337542/selection/1cf7ad2c8ba9afef7858414203f4593b25338cbc444eff9f31531226939ba2f2" "q-337542/selection/2929e3aa7077b26de26ed6f12808d2703d5dc866106455fbc861551c29f67c16"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-337542" :question/id #uuid "28a99110-2e19-556d-8656-9d4cd7895e05" :question/math-academy-id "q-337542" :question/problem "Consider the following differential equation.\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}-5{y}^{′}+6y=2x{e}^{2x}\n$$\n\nThe general solution to this equation can be written as\n\n$$\ny(x)=A{e}^{2x}+B{e}^{3x}+g(x),\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ and $B$ are constants. Which of the following could be the function $g(x)?$\n\n*Hint: Use the variation of parameters method. You may also wish to make use of the following result (integration constants omitted):*\n\n$$\n\\int x{e}^{-x}dx=-(x+1){e}^{-x}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have a second-order linear differential equation with linearly independent fundamental solutions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x).$ Then, according to the method of variation of parameters, we can seek a particular solution ${y}_{p}$ using the ansatz\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}(x) & ={u}_{1}(x){y}_{1}(x)+{u}_{2}(x){y}_{2}(x),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${u}_{1}$ and ${u}_{2}$ satisfy the following system:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}^{′}{y}_{1}^{′}+{u}_{2}^{′}{y}_{2}^{′}=f(x) \\\\ {u}_{1}^{′}{y}_{1}+{u}_{2}^{′}{y}_{2}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solution to this system is\n\n$$\n{u}_{1}(x)=-\\int \\frac{f(x){y}_{2}(x)}{W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})}\\,\\text{d}x,\\,{u}_{2}(x)=\\int \\frac{f(x){y}_{1}(x)}{W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})}\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nwhere $W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})$ is the Wronskian.\n\nFirst, we note that the forcing term is $f(x)=2x{e}^{2x}.$\n\nFrom the given information, we see that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{2x}+B{e}^{3x}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the fundamental solutions to the corresponding homogeneous equation are\n\n$$\n{y}_{1}(x)={e}^{2x},\\,{y}_{2}(x)={e}^{3x}.\n$$\n\nWe can use the method of variation of parameters if these solutions are independent. So, let's first compute the Wronskian:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({y}_{1},{y}_{2}) & =|\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{2}^{′}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & ={y}_{1}{y}_{2}^{′}-{y}_{1}^{′}{y}_{2} \\\\  & =({e}^{2x})({e}^{3x})^{′}-({e}^{2x})^{′}({e}^{3x}) \\\\  & =({e}^{2x})(3{e}^{3x})-(2{e}^{2x})({e}^{3x}) \\\\  & =3{e}^{5x}-2{e}^{5x} \\\\  & ={e}^{5x}\\ne 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the Wronskian is not equal to zero, this confirms that the functions  ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ are independent. \n\nLet's compute ${u}_{1}(x)$ and ${u}_{2}(x)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}(x) & =-\\int \\frac{2x{e}^{2x}\\cdot {e}^{3x}}{{e}^{5x}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\int 2x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-{x}^{2} \\\\ {u}_{2}(x) & =\\int \\frac{2x{e}^{2x}\\cdot {e}^{2x}}{{e}^{5x}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\int x{e}^{-x}\\text{ }\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-2(x+1){e}^{-x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}(x) & ={u}_{1}{y}_{1}+{u}_{2}{y}_{2} \\\\  & =(-{x}^{2})({e}^{2x})+[-2(x+1){e}^{-x}({e}^{3x})] \\\\  & =-{x}^{2}{e}^{2x}-2(x+1){e}^{2x} \\\\  & =-({x}^{2}+2x+2){e}^{2x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe general solution is the sum of the complementary and particular solutions, and can be written as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(x) & ={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x) \\\\  & =A{e}^{2x}+B{e}^{3x}-({x}^{2}+2x+2){e}^{2x} \\\\  & =(A-2){e}^{2x}+B{e}^{3x}-({x}^{2}+2x){e}^{2x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we redefine the arbitrary constant $A$ as\n\n$$\nA:=(A-2)\n$$\n\nour general solution becomes\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =A{e}^{2x}+B{e}^{3x}\\,{{-\\,({x}^{2}+2x){e}^{2x}}_{⏟}}_{g(x)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\ng(x)=-({x}^{2}+2x){e}^{2x}.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-337542/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b165305b-42f4-584a-9f8f-9455e5a05a9e"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-337542"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-337391"] :question/problem "Consider the following differential equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}-6{y}^{′}+9y=\\frac{2{e}^{3x}}{1+{x}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nThe general solution to this equation can be written as\n\n$$\ny(x)=A{e}^{3x}+Bx{e}^{3x}+g(x),\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ and $B$ are constants. Which of the following could be the function $g(x)?$\n\n*Hint: Use the variation of parameters method.*" :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have a second-order linear differential equation with linearly independent fundamental solutions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x).$ Then, according to the method of variation of parameters, we can seek a particular solution ${y}_{p}$ using the ansatz\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}(x) & ={u}_{1}(x){y}_{1}(x)+{u}_{2}(x){y}_{2}(x),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${u}_{1}$ and ${u}_{2}$ satisfy the following system:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}^{′}{y}_{1}^{′}+{u}_{2}^{′}{y}_{2}^{′}=f(x) \\\\ {u}_{1}^{′}{y}_{1}+{u}_{2}^{′}{y}_{2}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solution to this system is\n\n$$\n{u}_{1}(x)=-\\int \\frac{f(x){y}_{2}(x)}{W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})}\\,\\text{d}x,\\,{u}_{2}(x)=\\int \\frac{f(x){y}_{1}(x)}{W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})}\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nwhere $W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})$ is the Wronskian.\n\nFrom the given information, we see that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{3x}+Bx{e}^{3x}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the fundamental solutions to the corresponding homogeneous equation are\n\n$$\n{y}_{1}(x)={e}^{3x},\\,{y}_{2}(x)=x{e}^{3x}.\n$$\n\nWe can use the method of variation of parameters if these solutions are independent. So, let's first compute the Wronskian:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({y}_{1},{y}_{2}) & =|\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{2}^{′}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & ={y}_{1}{y}_{2}^{′}-{y}_{1}^{′}{y}_{2} \\\\  & =({e}^{3x})(x{e}^{3x})^{′}-({e}^{3x})^{′}(x{e}^{3x}) \\\\  & =({e}^{3x})({e}^{3x}+3x{e}^{3x})-(3{e}^{3x})(x{e}^{3x}) \\\\  & ={e}^{6x}+3x{e}^{6x}-3x{e}^{6x} \\\\  & ={e}^{6x}\\ne 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the Wronskian is not equal to zero, this confirms that the functions  ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ are independent.\n\nLet's compute ${u}_{1}(x)$ and ${u}_{2}(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}(x) & =-\\int \\frac{2{e}^{3x}\\cdot x{e}^{3x}}{(1+{x}^{2}){e}^{6x}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\int \\frac{2x}{1+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+{x}^{2}) \\\\ {u}_{2}(x) & =\\int \\frac{2{e}^{3x}\\cdot {e}^{3x}}{(1+{x}^{2}){e}^{6x}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\int \\frac{1}{1+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we needed to use substitution $t=1+{x}^{2}$ to find ${u}_{1}(x).$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}(x) & ={u}_{1}{y}_{1}+{u}_{2}{y}_{2} \\\\  & =({e}^{3x})(-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+{x}^{2}))+(x{e}^{3x})(2\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x) \\\\  & ={e}^{3x}(2x\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+{x}^{2})).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe general solution is the sum of the complementary and particular solutions, and can be written as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(x) & ={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x) \\\\  & =A{e}^{3x}+Bx{e}^{3x}+{{{e}^{3x}(2x\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+{x}^{2}))}_{⏟}}_{g(x)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\ng(x)={e}^{3x}(2x\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+{x}^{2})).\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "q-338528/selection/e97f9003b87c878e3cfa5b76844eea2e08c518fbd132e72906c088e6d3701cce" :answer/id #uuid "cf6471cc-b71a-5817-8290-097d974bcf9c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{8}\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x\\operatorname{sec}⁡2x" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-338528/selection/54ec2da7aad4e19a28cc491a4a42d4098dc5bbc3699390fe06c558fb2799d7df" :answer/id #uuid "227c5b6f-0c99-58f0-a784-abc08f190577" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x\\operatorname{csc}⁡2x" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-338528/selection/080dab11782f78f2f4cd97fb0a7423e589e681fbc1b20e093ff9a14417bd0072" :answer/id #uuid "bd9029a9-fbd0-500a-a64e-34192a9548dd" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x\\operatorname{sec}⁡2x" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-338528/selection/87e2b1f2118daafad1d3d6cc3c983e92e758255d033aa37c349666c2eb747a48" :answer/id #uuid "80b444f0-2564-5163-aa4f-e5e8bb5c1ef0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{8}\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x\\operatorname{csc}⁡2x" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-338528/selection/5060fc6b515b147348752feeaeebcacb116d0f4be26865fec6627d2394b91efe" :answer/id #uuid "d72a261d-8fcd-594f-a08b-c2895688f990" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x\\operatorname{csc}⁡2x" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-338528/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "cffb04b3-0202-53e2-9cac-2592580f6980" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-338528/selection/87e2b1f2118daafad1d3d6cc3c983e92e758255d033aa37c349666c2eb747a48" :answer-field/choices ["q-338528/selection/e97f9003b87c878e3cfa5b76844eea2e08c518fbd132e72906c088e6d3701cce" "q-338528/selection/54ec2da7aad4e19a28cc491a4a42d4098dc5bbc3699390fe06c558fb2799d7df" "q-338528/selection/080dab11782f78f2f4cd97fb0a7423e589e681fbc1b20e093ff9a14417bd0072" "q-338528/selection/87e2b1f2118daafad1d3d6cc3c983e92e758255d033aa37c349666c2eb747a48" "q-338528/selection/5060fc6b515b147348752feeaeebcacb116d0f4be26865fec6627d2394b91efe"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-338528" :question/id #uuid "320207c2-9021-5944-819c-2e3cc83910e2" :question/math-academy-id "q-338528" :question/problem "Consider the following differential equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+4y={csc}^{3}⁡2x.\n$$\n\nThe general solution to this equation can be written as\n\n$$\ny(x)=P\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+Q\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x+g(x),\n$$\n\nwhere $P$ and $Q$ are constants. Which of the following could be the function $g(x)?$\n\n*Hint: Use the variation of parameters method. You may also wish to make use of the following result:*\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x=2{cos}^{2}⁡2x-1\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have a second-order linear differential equation with linearly independent fundamental solutions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x).$ Then, according to the method of variation of parameters, we can seek a particular solution ${y}_{p}$ using the ansatz\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}(x) & ={u}_{1}(x){y}_{1}(x)+{u}_{2}(x){y}_{2}(x),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${u}_{1}$ and ${u}_{2}$ satisfy the following system:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}^{′}{y}_{1}^{′}+{u}_{2}^{′}{y}_{2}^{′}=f(x) \\\\ {u}_{1}^{′}{y}_{1}+{u}_{2}^{′}{y}_{2}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solution to this system is\n\n$$\n{u}_{1}(x)=-\\int \\frac{f(x){y}_{2}(x)}{W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})}\\,dx,\\,{u}_{2}(x)=\\int \\frac{f(x){y}_{1}(x)}{W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})}\\,dx,\n$$\n\nwhere $W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})$ is the Wronskian.\n\nFirst, we note that the forcing term is $f(x)={csc}^{3}⁡2x.$\n\nFrom the given information, we see that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}(x)=P\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+Q\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the fundamental solutions to the corresponding homogeneous equation are\n\n$$\n{y}_{1}(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x,\\,{y}_{2}(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x.\n$$\n\nWe can use the method of variation of parameters if these solutions are independent. So, let's first compute the Wronskian:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({y}_{1},{y}_{2}) & =|\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{2}^{′}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & ={y}_{1}(x){y}_{2}^{′}(x)-{y}_{1}^{′}(x){y}_{2}(x) \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x)(\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x)^{′}-(\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x)^{′}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x) \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x)(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x)-(-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x)(\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x) \\\\  & =2{cos}^{2}⁡2x+2{sin}^{2}⁡2x \\\\  & =2\\ne 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the Wronskian is not equal to zero, this confirms that the functions  ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ are independent. \n\nLet's compute ${u}_{1}(x)$ and ${u}_{2}(x)$:\n\n- First, we compute ${u}_{1}(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}(x) & =-\\int \\frac{{csc}^{3}⁡2x\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡2x}{2}\\,dx \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\int {csc}^{2}⁡2x\\,dx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{cot}⁡2x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Then, we compute ${u}_{2}(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{2}(x) & =\\int \\frac{{csc}^{3}⁡2x\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡2x}{2}\\,dx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\int \\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x}{{sin}^{3}⁡2x}\\,dx\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $u=\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x.$ Then $du=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x\\,dx,$ so $\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x\\,dx=\\frac{1}{2}\\,du,$ and the integral becomes\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}\\int \\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x}{{sin}^{3}⁡2x}\\,dx & =\\frac{1}{2}\\int \\frac{1}{{u}^{3}}(\\frac{1}{2}\\,du) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\int {u}^{-3}\\,du \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{8}{u}^{-2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{8{sin}^{2}⁡2x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}(x) & ={u}_{1}{y}_{1}+{u}_{2}{y}_{2} \\\\  & =(\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{cot}⁡2x)(\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x)+(-\\frac{1}{8{sin}^{2}⁡2x})(\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x) \\\\  & =\\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡2x}{4\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x}-\\frac{1}{8\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x}(2{cos}^{2}⁡2x-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x}{8\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x\\,\\operatorname{csc}⁡2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe general solution is the sum of the complementary and particular solutions, and can be written as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(x) & ={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x) \\\\  & =P\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+Q\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x\\,+\\,{{\\frac{1}{8}\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x\\operatorname{csc}⁡2x}_{⏟}}_{g(x)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\ng(x)=\\frac{1}{8}\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x\\operatorname{csc}⁡2x.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-338528/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5b7c316a-2ec9-5f4c-853c-e82b09755db0"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-338528"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-337950"] :question/problem "Consider the following differential equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+4y=2\\operatorname{tan}⁡x.\n$$\n\nThe general solution to this equation can be written as\n\n$$\ny(x)=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x+g(x),\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ and $B$ are constants. Which of the following could be the function $g(x)?$\n\n*Hint: Use the variation of parameters method. You may also wish to make use of the following results (integration constants omitted):*\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡2x\\operatorname{tan}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x & =x-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x \\\\ \\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡2x\\operatorname{tan}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\operatorname{cos}⁡x|-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x-\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have a second-order linear differential equation with linearly independent fundamental solutions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x).$ Then, according to the method of variation of parameters, we can seek a particular solution ${y}_{p}$ using the ansatz\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}(x) & ={u}_{1}(x){y}_{1}(x)+{u}_{2}(x){y}_{2}(x),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${u}_{1}$ and ${u}_{2}$ satisfy the following system:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}^{′}{y}_{1}^{′}+{u}_{2}^{′}{y}_{2}^{′}=f(x) \\\\ {u}_{1}^{′}{y}_{1}+{u}_{2}^{′}{y}_{2}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solution to this system is\n\n$$\n{u}_{1}(x)=-\\int \\frac{f(x){y}_{2}(x)}{W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})}\\,\\text{d}x,\\,{u}_{2}(x)=\\int \\frac{f(x){y}_{1}(x)}{W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})}\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nwhere $W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})$ is the Wronskian.\n\nFirst, we note that the forcing term is $f(x)=2\\operatorname{tan}⁡x.$\n\nFrom the given information, we see that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}(x)=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the fundamental solutions to the corresponding homogeneous equation are\n\n$$\n{y}_{1}(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x,\\,{y}_{2}(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x.\n$$\n\nWe can use the method of variation of parameters if these solutions are linearly independent. So, let's first compute the Wronskian:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({y}_{1},{y}_{2}) & =|\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{2}^{′}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & ={y}_{1}(x){y}_{2}^{′}(x)-{y}_{1}^{′}(x){y}_{2}(x) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x(\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x)^{′}-(\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x)^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x \\\\  & =2{cos}^{2}⁡2x+2{sin}^{2}⁡2x \\\\  & =2\\ne 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the Wronskian is not equal to zero, this confirms that the functions  ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ are independent. \n\nLet's compute ${u}_{1}(x)$ and ${u}_{2}(x)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}(x) & =-\\int \\frac{f(x){y}_{2}(x)}{W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\int \\frac{2\\operatorname{tan}⁡x\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡2x}{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡2x\\operatorname{tan}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x-x \\\\ {u}_{2}(x) & =\\int \\frac{f(x){y}_{1}(x)}{W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{2\\operatorname{tan}⁡x\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡2x}{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡2x\\operatorname{tan}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\operatorname{cos}⁡x|-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x-\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}(x) & ={u}_{1}{y}_{1}+{u}_{2}{y}_{2} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x(\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x-x)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x(\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\operatorname{cos}⁡x|-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x-\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =-x\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\operatorname{cos}⁡x|-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe general solution is the sum of the complementary and particular solutions, and can be written as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & ={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x) \\\\  & =A\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x-x\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\operatorname{cos}⁡x|-\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x \\\\  & =A\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+(B-\\frac{1}{2})\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x-x\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\operatorname{cos}⁡x|\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we redefine the arbitrary constant $B$ as\n\n$$\nB:=(B-\\frac{1}{2})\n$$\n\nour general solution becomes\n\n$$\ny=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x\\,{{-x\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\operatorname{cos}⁡x|}_{⏟}}_{g(x)}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\ng(x)=-x\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\operatorname{cos}⁡x|.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "q-338774/selection/09777b2c54b62e1e4304608931dc270d792a883dc0e9514e59cf975a04439b40" :answer/id #uuid "591936b0-73c0-5cbe-9c61-86106b4f2f48" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{e}^{-x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\operatorname{cos}⁡x|-x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-338774/selection/9d661053fb16580c8543d183491592549344a728955b58f2aa83c06238204a5c" :answer/id #uuid "6ed31006-e747-5a19-93dc-7c3d3751e4f5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{e}^{-x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\operatorname{cos}⁡x|+x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-338774/selection/7f7b4282fe61045330d45614a9b10350f3925310099f8e794a85e1b6d8cc9247" :answer/id #uuid "6860da19-ba72-51a4-9aef-ec2a8bc8e60e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x{e}^{-x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\operatorname{cos}⁡x|-x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-338774/selection/6b3abf266fbc4b702018d937769b84395f2921985d4ba0134e061bfb52973f62" :answer/id #uuid "9dc4595e-549c-561b-8030-1233c4b6d192" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x{e}^{-x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\operatorname{cos}⁡x|+x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-338774/selection/f5e2843dbdb7f624aae51d359794714e4b2f82d99454a6d398dda13c8979d4ed" :answer/id #uuid "3f84b1b9-c4f7-556c-9f6a-464cba731885" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{e}^{-x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\operatorname{cos}⁡x|+x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-338774/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "0d485d7d-8d0f-51cf-9ee0-47ed5776a788" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-338774/selection/f5e2843dbdb7f624aae51d359794714e4b2f82d99454a6d398dda13c8979d4ed" :answer-field/choices ["q-338774/selection/09777b2c54b62e1e4304608931dc270d792a883dc0e9514e59cf975a04439b40" "q-338774/selection/9d661053fb16580c8543d183491592549344a728955b58f2aa83c06238204a5c" "q-338774/selection/7f7b4282fe61045330d45614a9b10350f3925310099f8e794a85e1b6d8cc9247" "q-338774/selection/6b3abf266fbc4b702018d937769b84395f2921985d4ba0134e061bfb52973f62" "q-338774/selection/f5e2843dbdb7f624aae51d359794714e4b2f82d99454a6d398dda13c8979d4ed"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-338774" :question/id #uuid "9fb5263d-b905-5758-8aab-2336a62acfc9" :question/math-academy-id "q-338774" :question/problem "Consider the following differential equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+2{y}^{′}+2y={e}^{-x}\\operatorname{sec}⁡x.\n$$\n\nThe general solution to this equation can be written as\n\n$$\ny(x)={e}^{-x}(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)+g(x),\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ and $B$ are constants. Which of the following could be the function $g(x)?$\n\n*Hint: Use the variation of parameters method. You may also wish to make use of the following result (integration constant omitted):*\n\n$$\n\\int \\operatorname{tan}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x=-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\operatorname{cos}⁡x|\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have a second-order linear differential equation with linearly independent fundamental solutions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x).$ Then, according to the method of variation of parameters, we can seek a particular solution ${y}_{p}$ using the ansatz\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}(x) & ={u}_{1}(x){y}_{1}(x)+{u}_{2}(x){y}_{2}(x),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${u}_{1}$ and ${u}_{2}$ satisfy the following system:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}^{′}{y}_{1}^{′}+{u}_{2}^{′}{y}_{2}^{′}=f(x) \\\\ {u}_{1}^{′}{y}_{1}+{u}_{2}^{′}{y}_{2}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solution to this system is\n\n$$\n{u}_{1}(x)=-\\int \\frac{f(x){y}_{2}(x)}{W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})}\\,dx,\\,{u}_{2}(x)=\\int \\frac{f(x){y}_{1}(x)}{W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})}\\,dx,\n$$\n\nwhere $W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})$ is the Wronskian.\n\nFirst, we note that the forcing term is $f(x)={e}^{-x}\\operatorname{sec}⁡x.$\n\nFrom the given information, we see that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}(x)={e}^{-x}(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡x).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the fundamental solutions to the corresponding homogeneous equation are\n\n$$\n{y}_{1}(x)={e}^{-x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x,\\,{y}_{2}(x)={e}^{-x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\n$$\n\nWe can use the method of variation of parameters if these solutions are linearly independent. So, let's first compute the Wronskian:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({y}_{1},{y}_{2}) & =|\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{2}^{′}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & ={y}_{1}(x){y}_{2}^{′}(x)-{y}_{1}^{′}(x){y}_{2}(x) \\\\  & =({e}^{-x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)({e}^{-x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)^{′}-({e}^{-x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)^{′}({e}^{-x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x) \\\\  & =({e}^{-x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)(-{e}^{-x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+{e}^{-x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)-(-{e}^{-x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-{e}^{-x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)({e}^{-x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x) \\\\  & ={e}^{-2x}({cos}^{2}⁡x+{sin}^{2}⁡x) \\\\  & ={e}^{-2x}\\ne 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the Wronskian is not equal to zero, this confirms that the functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ are independent.\n\nLet’s compute ${u}_{1}(x)$ and ${u}_{2}(x)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}(x) & =-\\int \\frac{f(x){y}_{2}(x)}{W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})}\\,dx \\\\  & =-\\int \\frac{{e}^{-x}\\operatorname{sec}⁡x\\cdot {e}^{-x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{{e}^{-2x}}\\,dx \\\\  & =-\\int \\operatorname{tan}⁡x\\,dx \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\operatorname{cos}⁡x| \\\\ {u}_{2}(x) & =\\int \\frac{f(x){y}_{1}(x)}{W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})}\\,dx \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{{e}^{-x}\\operatorname{sec}⁡x\\cdot {e}^{-x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{{e}^{-2x}}\\,dx \\\\  & =\\int dx \\\\  & =x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}(x) & ={u}_{1}{y}_{1}+{u}_{2}{y}_{2} \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\operatorname{cos}⁡x|)({e}^{-x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)+(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)(x{e}^{-x}) \\\\  & ={e}^{-x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\operatorname{cos}⁡x|+x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe general solution is the sum of the complementary and particular solutions, and can be written as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(x) & ={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x) \\\\  & ={e}^{-x}(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)+{{{e}^{-x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\operatorname{cos}⁡x|+x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)}_{⏟}}_{g(x)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\ng(x)={e}^{-x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\operatorname{cos}⁡x|+x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x).\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-338774/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5b7c316a-2ec9-5f4c-853c-e82b09755db0"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-338774"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-6618"] :question/problem "Consider the following differential equation.\n\n$$\ny'' - 2y' + y = 18x e^{x}\n$$\n\nThe general solution to this equation can be written as\n\n$$\ny(x) = A e^{x} + B x e^{x} + g(x),\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ and $B$ are constants. What could be the function $g(x)?$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have a second-order linear differential equation with linearly independent fundamental solutions $y_1(x)$ and $y_2(x)$. Then, according to the method of variation of parameters, we can seek a particular solution $y_p$ may be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}(x) & ={u}_{1}(x){y}_{1}(x)+{u}_{2}(x){y}_{2}(x),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $u_1$ and $u_2$ satisfy the following system:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}^{′}{y}_{1}^{′}+{u}_{2}^{′}{y}_{2}^{′}=f(x) \\\\ {u}_{1}^{′}{y}_{1}+{u}_{2}^{′}{y}_{2}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solution to this system is\n\n$$\nu_1(x) = -\\int \\dfrac{f(x)y_2(x)}{W(y_1,y_2)}\\,dx,\n\\qquad\nu_2(x) = \\int \\dfrac{f(x)y_1(x)}{W(y_1,y_2)}\\,dx,\n$$\n\nwhere $W(y_1,y_2)$ is the Wronskian.\n\nFrom the given information, we see that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\ny_c(x) = A e^{x} + B x e^{x}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the fundamental solutions to the corresponding homogeneous equation are\n\n$$\ny_1(x) = e^{x}, \\qquad y_2(x) = x e^{x}.\n$$\n\nWe can use the method of variation of parameters if these solutions are independent. So, let's first compute the Wronskian:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({y}_{1},{y}_{2}) & =|\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{2}^{′}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & ={y}_{1}{y}_{2}^{′}-{y}_{1}^{′}{y}_{2} \\\\  & =({e}^{x})(x{e}^{x})^{′}-({e}^{x})^{′}(x{e}^{x}) \\\\  & =({e}^{x})({e}^{x}+x{e}^{x})-({e}^{x})(x{e}^{x}) \\\\  & ={e}^{2x}+x{e}^{2x}-x{e}^{2x} \\\\  & ={e}^{2x}\\ne 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the Wronskian is not equal to zero, this confirms that the functions  $y_1(x)$ and $y_2(x)$ are independent.\n\nNow compute $u_1(x)$ and $u_2(x)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}(x) & =-\\int \\frac{18x{e}^{x}\\cdot x{e}^{x}}{{e}^{2x}}\\,dx \\\\  & =-18\\int {x}^{2}\\,dx \\\\  & =-6{x}^{3} \\\\ {u}_{2}(x) & =\\int \\frac{18x{e}^{x}\\cdot {e}^{x}}{{e}^{2x}}\\,dx \\\\  & =18\\int x\\,dx \\\\  & =9{x}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}(x) & ={u}_{1}{y}_{1}+{u}_{2}{y}_{2} \\\\  & =(-6{x}^{3}){e}^{x}+(9{x}^{2})(x{e}^{x}) \\\\  & =-6{x}^{3}{e}^{x}+9{x}^{3}{e}^{x} \\\\  & =3{x}^{3}{e}^{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe general solution is the sum of the complementary and particular solutions, and can be written as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(x) & ={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x) \\\\  & =A{e}^{x}+Bx{e}^{x}+{{3{x}^{3}{e}^{x}}_{⏟}}_{g(x)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\ng(x) = 3x^3 e^{x}.\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-6617"] :question/problem "Consider the following differential equation\n\n$$\ny''+y=\\tan{x}.\n$$\n\nThe general solution to this equation can be written as\n\n$$\ny(x)=A\\cos x + B\\sin x + g(x),\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ and $B$ are constants. What could be the function $g(x)?$\n\n*Hint: Use the variation of parameters method. You may also wish to make use of the following result (integration constant omitted):*\n\n$$\n\\int \\tan x \\sin x\\,\\textrm{d}x\n= -\\sin x + \\ln\\!\\left|\\tan x+\\sec x\\right|\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have a second-order linear differential equation with linearly independent fundamental solutions $y_1(x)$ and $y_2(x).$ Then, according to the method of variation of parameters, we can seek a particular solution $y_p$ using the ansatz\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}(x) & ={u}_{1}(x){y}_{1}(x)+{u}_{2}(x){y}_{2}(x),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $u_1$ and $u_2$ satisfy the following system:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}^{′}{y}_{1}^{′}+{u}_{2}^{′}{y}_{2}^{′}=f(x) \\\\ {u}_{1}^{′}{y}_{1}+{u}_{2}^{′}{y}_{2}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solution to this system is\n\n$$\nu_1(x)=-\\displaystyle\\int\\dfrac{f(x)y_2(x)}{W(y_1,y_2)}\\,\\mathrm{d}x,\n\\qquad\nu_2(x)=\\displaystyle\\int\\dfrac{f(x)y_1(x)}{W(y_1,y_2)}\\,\\mathrm{d}x,\n$$\n\nwhere $W(y_1,y_2)$ is the Wronskian.\n\nFirst, we note that the forcing term is $f(x)=\\tan x.$\n\nFrom the given information, we see that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\ny_c(x)=A\\cos x + B\\sin x.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the fundamental solutions to the corresponding homogeneous equation are\n\n$$\ny_1(x)=\\cos x,\n\\qquad\ny_2(x)=\\sin x.\n$$\n\nWe can use the method of variation of parameters if these solutions are linearly independent. So, let's first compute the Wronskian:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({y}_{1},{y}_{2}) & =|\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{2}^{′}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & ={y}_{1}(x){y}_{2}^{′}(x)-{y}_{1}^{′}(x){y}_{2}(x) \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)^{′}-(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)^{′}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x) \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)-(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x) \\\\  & ={cos}^{2}⁡x+{sin}^{2}⁡x \\\\  & =1\\ne 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the Wronskian is not equal to zero, this confirms that the functions $y_1(x)$ and $y_2(x)$ are independent.\n\nLet’s compute $u_1(x)$ and $u_2(x)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}(x) & =-\\int \\frac{f(x){y}_{2}(x)}{W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})}\\,dx \\\\  & =-\\int \\operatorname{tan}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,dx \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-ln\\,|\\operatorname{tan}⁡x+\\operatorname{sec}⁡x| \\\\ {u}_{2}(x) & =\\int \\frac{f(x){y}_{1}(x)}{W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})}\\,dx \\\\  & =\\int \\operatorname{tan}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,dx \\\\  & =\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,dx \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}(x) & ={u}_{1}{y}_{1}+{u}_{2}{y}_{2} \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)\\,(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-ln\\,|\\operatorname{tan}⁡x+\\operatorname{sec}⁡x|)+(-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x) \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{cos}⁡xln\\,|\\operatorname{tan}⁡x+\\operatorname{sec}⁡x|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe general solution is the sum of the complementary and particular solutions, and can be written as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(x) & ={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x) \\\\  & =A\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\,{{-\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡xln\\,|\\operatorname{tan}⁡x+\\operatorname{sec}⁡x|}_{⏟}}_{g(x)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\ng(x)=-\\cos x\\ln\\!\\left|\\tan x+\\sec x\\right|.\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]}]
